# ADS-B API Client

A small Python client for the authenticated ADS-B sample exchange API.

## Requirements

- Python 3.10 or later
- An API base URL, username, and bearer token

## Install

Install from a local checkout:

```sh
python -m pip install .
```

The distribution is named `adsb-client` and the Python package is imported as
`adsb_client`.

## Usage
Prior to using the client, you must obtain a username and bearer token from the
API server. The client is initialized with the base URL, username, and token.

```bash
set -a
export ADSB_BASE_URL="https://api.example.com"
export ADSB_USERNAME="your-username"
export ADSB_TOKEN="your-token"
set +a
```
Then in Python:
```python
from adsb_client import ADSBClient, required_env

client = ADSBClient(
    base_url=required_env("ADSB_BASE_URL"),
    username=required_env("ADSB_USERNAME"),
    token=required_env("ADSB_TOKEN"),
)

client.post_results([bytes.fromhex("8D392AE258B501C727E8460EA386")])
```
## Radio samples

`get_iq_samples()` returns the last 0.5 s received by the radio as a numpy
array of complex numbers (`complex64`): a 1090 MHz carrier sampled at 4 MHz,
i.e. 2,000,000 samples. The server captures a new buffer every 0.5 s.

```python
from adsb_client import ADSBClient, required_env

client = ADSBClient(
    base_url=required_env("ADSB_BASE_URL"),
    username=required_env("ADSB_USERNAME"),
    token=required_env("ADSB_TOKEN"),
)

x = client.get_iq_samples()  # numpy.ndarray, dtype complex64, 2,000,000 samples
print(x.dtype, x.shape, abs(x).max())
```

`get_iq_buffer()` returns the same samples with their description:

```python
buffer = client.get_iq_buffer()
x = buffer.samples  # numpy complex64 array
print(buffer.buffer_id, buffer.capture_time, buffer.sample_rate, buffer.center_frequency)
buffer.save("buffer.iq")  # reload with numpy.fromfile("buffer.iq", numpy.complex64)

frames = [bytes.fromhex("8D392AE258B501C727E8460EA386")]  # frames decoded from x
client.post_results(frames, frame_hash=buffer.sample_hash)
```

| Attribute | Content |
|---|---|
| `samples` | numpy `complex64` array of the samples |
| `buffer_id` | buffer number, incremented every 0.5 s |
| `capture_time` | UTC time of the first sample (approximate) |
| `center_frequency`, `sample_rate` | 1090e6 Hz and 4e6 Hz |
| `duration` | 0.5 s |
| `sample_hash` | SHA-256 of the samples, to pass as `frame_hash` with the decoded frames |

When the radio capture is stopped, both methods raise
`SamplesUnavailableError`. The client checks the size and the SHA-256 of what
it receives and raises `IOError` if the download is incomplete.
